comments.message:
This won't match on `definitely_not_atoi`. It's using AST identifiers rather than text matching. Here's an example to confirm:

```
  $ cat test.cpp  
  int a = atoi("42");
  int b = std::atoi("42");
  int c = definitely_not_atoi("42");

  $ ast-grep scan --rule common-errors.yml test.cpp
  error[unsafe-int-parse]:
    4 │     int a = atoi("42");
      │             ^^^^^^^^^^
  error[unsafe-int-parse]:
    5 │     int b = std::atoi("42");
      │             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

Is there a way to declare this all as one pattern?  It seems unlikely, but 

json.dump("error_handler_t::replace");

Would succeed, even though the argument is in the wrong position and is a quoted string.
